@anon.3c5d4856225436a016f648d8ca8332b3.2581 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ [2569 x i8] }> <{ [2569 x i8] c"# `ffi_pure`\0A\0AThe tracking issue for this feature is: [#58329]\0A\0A------\0A\0AThe `#[ffi_pure]` attribute applies clang's `pure` attribute to foreign\0Afunctions declarations.\0A\0AThat is, `#[ffi_pure]` functions shall have no effects except for its return\0Avalue, which shall not change across two consecutive function calls with\0Athe same parameters.\0A\0AApplying the `#[ffi_pure]` attribute to a function that violates these\0Arequirements is undefined behavior.\0A\0AThis attribute enables Rust to perform common optimizations, like sub-expression\0Aelimination and loop optimizations. Some common examples of pure functions are\0A`strlen` or `memcmp`.\0A\0AThese optimizations are only applicable when the compiler can prove that no\0Aprogram state observable by the `#[ffi_pure]` function has changed between calls\0Aof the function, which could alter the result. See also the `#[ffi_const]`\0Aattribute, which provides stronger guarantees regarding the allowable behavior\0Aof a function, enabling further optimization.\0A\0A## Pitfalls\0A\0AA `#[ffi_pure]` function can read global memory through the function\0Aparameters (e.g. pointers), globals, etc. `#[ffi_pure]` functions are not\0Areferentially-transparent, and are therefore more relaxed than `#[ffi_const]`\0Afunctions.\0A\0AHowever, accessing global memory through volatile or atomic reads can violate the\0Arequirement that two consecutive function calls shall return the same value.\0A\0AA `pure` function that returns unit has no effect on the abstract machine's\0Astate.\0A\0AA `#[ffi_pure]` function must not diverge, neither via a side effect (e.g. a\0Acall to `abort`) nor by infinite loops.\0A\0AWhen translating C headers to Rust FFI, it is worth verifying for which targets\0Athe `pure` attribute is enabled in those headers, and using the appropriate\0A`cfg` macros in the Rust side to match those definitions. While the semantics of\0A`pure` are implemented identically by many C and C++ compilers, e.g., clang,\0A[GCC], [ARM C/C++ compiler], [IBM ILE C/C++], etc. they are not necessarily\0Aimplemented in this way on all of them. It is therefore also worth verifying\0Athat the semantics of the C toolchain used to compile the binary being linked\0Aagainst are compatible with those of the `#[ffi_pure]`.\0A\0A\0A[#58329]: https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/58329\0A[ARM C/C++ compiler]: http://infocenter.arm.com/help/index.jsp?topic=/com.arm.doc.dui0491c/Cacigdac.html\0A[GCC]: https://gcc.gnu.org/onlinedocs/gcc/Common-Function-Attributes.html#index-pure-function-attribute\0A[IBM ILE C/C++]: https://www.ibm.com/support/knowledgecenter/fr/ssw_ibm_i_71/rzarg/fn_attrib_pure.htm\0A" }>, align 1
